Below is a tiny **Greasemonkey** module that watches for outgoing HTTP requests to a chosen set of API endpoints and logs each call.  
When a request matches one of the listed URLs, the script prints a small JSON object that contains the timestamp, the full URL, the HTTP method and any headers that were sent.  
You can later read those logs to create a short “inner workings” document for the site.

### 1. manifest.json (the extension’s manifest)

```json
{
    "manifest_version": 2,
    "name": "API Endpoint Observer",
    "version": "1.0",
    "description": "Monitors specified web‑API calls and records them.",
    "permissions": ["activeTab"],
    "background": {
        "scripts": ["index.js"]
    },
    "scripts": [
        {
            "function": "onRequest",
            "args": [],
            "event": "document:request",
            "prevent": false,
            "source": "",
            "context": "",
            "code": "// This snippet runs when a new request is fired by the page.\n// It checks whether the target URL belongs to the watch‑list.\n// If it does, the details are logged.\n\nconst WATCHED_URLS = [\n    'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts/1',\n    'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/comments/1'\n]; // add more URLs here\n\nfunction logRequest(url, method, headers) {\n    const entry = {\n        timestamp: new Date().toISOString(),\n        url,\n        method,\n        headers: headers || {}\n    };\n    console.log('Logged request:', JSON.stringify(entry));\n    // Optional: persist the entry in localStorage for later processing\n    // localStorage.setItem('apiLogs', JSON.stringify([entry]));\n}\n\n// Attach the listener inside the Greasemonkey environment.\nwindow.Greasemonkey.on('request', function(request) {\n    const url = request.url;\n    const method = request.method;\n    const headers = request.headers || {};\n    if (WATCHED_URLS.includes(url)) {\n        logRequest(url, method, headers);\n    }\n});\n"
        }
    ]
}
```

### 2. index.js (the script that actually runs)

```js
// index.js – run through Greasemonkey
(function () {
    'use strict';

    // ---- list of target API URLs ------------------------------------
    const monitoredUrls = [
        'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts/1',
        'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/comments/1'
    ]; // feel free to add more entries

    // ---- helper that builds a log record -----------------------------
    function logRequest(url, method, headers) {
        const entry = {
            timestamp: new Date().toISOString(),
            url,
            method,
            headers: headers || {}
        };
        console.log('Request logged:', JSON.stringify(entry));
        // Uncomment the next line if you want the data saved in localStorage:
        // localStorage.setItem('apiLogs', JSON.stringify([entry]));
    }

    // ---- Greasemonkey event for every incoming request ---------------
    window.Greasemonkey.on('request', function (request) {
        const url = request.url;
        const method = request.method;
        const headers = request.headers || {};

        if (monitoredUrls.includes(url)) {
            logRequest(url, method, headers);
        }
    });
})();
```

### How to install

1. Save the two snippets above as **`manifest.json`** and **`index.js`** in a folder (e.g., `~/.config/greasemonkey/` or any directory you prefer).  
2. Open **Greasemonkey** (Chrome extension manager) and reload it.  
3. You will see new lines printed in the console whenever the page makes a call to one of the URLs listed in `monitoredUrls`.  

After a few minutes you can copy those logs into a text file or feed them to a simple script that turns each entry into a markdown page describing the endpoint, the HTTP method, headers, and the time of the call. This gives you a living documentation of the site’s inner workings without writing extra code.  

Feel free to expand `monitoredUrls` with the real endpoints you care about, and the observer will keep growing automatically. Happy documenting!
